# Voice profile — {{display_name}}

> How {{display_name}} writes outward comms (tracker comments, chat, PR bodies). `/ship` reads this
> as a **style guide** when it drafts, so the draft already sounds like you and needs less editing.
> **Personal data** — it's your writing fingerprint. Edit it freely; `/ship` also *proposes* updates
> from the edits you make at the ship review (you approve each one).
>
> This guide only shapes *phrasing*. It can never bend the hard comms rails (see "Do-not-override").

## Tone & register
- Formality: <e.g. plain and direct; contractions fine; not stiff>
- Warmth: <e.g. friendly but brief; a quick thanks when someone unblocked you>
- Directness: <e.g. lead with the answer, then the why>

## Structural habits
- Bullets vs prose: <e.g. bullets for multi-part results; a sentence or two otherwise>
- Greeting / sign-off: <e.g. no greeting in-thread; sign off with just my first name on email>
- Emoji: <e.g. none in tracker comments; a single ✅ ok in chat>
- Sentence length: <e.g. short; one idea per sentence>

## Diction
- Favored phrasings: <e.g. "here's what I found", "net:", "to confirm">
- Banned words / tics: <e.g. no "utilize" (use "use"), no em dashes, no "just wanted to">

## Examples (short, approved)
> <2-3 one-line examples in your own voice — how you'd actually open a results comment,
>  flag a caveat, or hand something off. Keep them short; never paste a full confidential thread.>

## Do-not-override (hard comms rails — voice bends phrasing, never these)
These come from `stack.yaml` / the policies and always win over any style preference above:
- **word_limits** — tracker comment / chat / PR / ticket caps are hard ceilings.
- **hyperlink_everything** — every ticket id, file, and PR is a smart link.
- **business-first, segmented** — lead with the business result; segment with counts / % / $.
- **stakeholder include-list** — `always_include` (and `include_self` if set) are non-negotiable.

